VerticalShakeTwice and VerticalShakeLowTwice walk sVerticalShakeData, whose end row is {-1, 0}.
On that row each computes (var6 - data[6]) * var5 / var6 with var6 == 0; data[6] is 0 there,
since every row change resets it. The result goes unused: the next test sees -1 and ends the
animation. The ROM links libgcc 14.2.1, whose __divsi3 returns without a fault on a zero divisor
(0 for 0 / 0, 0x7fffffff for a positive numerator, 0x80000000 for a negative one) and then calls
__aeabi_idiv0, a bare return. WebAssembly's i32.div_s traps on a zero divisor, so the module
stopped with "divide by zero" at the end of either animation. The fixtures 995161 (far and near
Tauros), 993907, 994086 and 994176 (near Tyranitar) sent out such a species.
Under IRV_WASM a zero divisor gives 0, the GBA's result for 0 / 0; every other row divides as
before.

diff --git a/src/pokemon_animation.c b/src/pokemon_animation.c
--- a/src/pokemon_animation.c
+++ b/src/pokemon_animation.c
@@ -1240,7 +1240,12 @@ static void VerticalShakeTwice(struct Sprite *sprite)
     u8 amplitude = 0;
 
     if (var5 != (u8)-2)
+#ifdef IRV_WASM
+        // The end row {-1, 0} lasts 0 frames: the GBA's __divsi3 answers 0 / 0 with 0, WebAssembly traps.
+        amplitude = var6 == 0 ? 0 : (var6 - var7) * var5 / var6;
+#else
         amplitude = (var6 - var7) * var5 / var6;
+#endif
     else
         amplitude = 0;
 
@@ -3671,7 +3676,12 @@ static void VerticalShakeLowTwice(struct Sprite *sprite)
     var6 = sVerticalShakeData[sprite->data[5]][1];
     var7 = 0;
     if (sVerticalShakeData[sprite->data[5]][0] != (u8)-2)
+#ifdef IRV_WASM
+        // The end row {-1, 0} lasts 0 frames: the GBA's __divsi3 answers 0 / 0 with 0, WebAssembly traps.
+        var7 = var6 == 0 ? 0 : (var6 - var9) * var5 / var6;
+#else
         var7 = (var6 - var9) * var5 / var6;
+#endif
     else
         var7 = 0;
 
